$DIR/main.p:37:11: p holds a view of s (&s, line 35), which was released at the end of its block at line 36, so p can no longer be used here
    p looks at memory that s holds, without owning it: the view was made by &s
    at line 35. A view of a local variable whose type has drop glue may be used
    only until the variable's scope ends, where the variable is released. At
    line 36 s was released at the end of its block, so the view may look at
    memory that was released, or at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Declare s in a block that encloses every use of p, or use p only inside the
    block of s
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)

