$DIR/main.p:95:2: s is released at the end of its block here, but a view of it (&s, line 94) was stored in *pw at line 94, where the compiler cannot check its uses
    A view of a local variable whose type has drop glue may be used only until
    the variable's scope ends, where the variable is released. The compiler
    follows views through local variables and their fields, and into the
    arguments of the calls that store them there, but the view of s made by &s
    at line 94 went into memory it does not follow, so it cannot check that the
    view is not used after line 95.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Keep the view in a local variable or a field of one, where the compiler
    follows it, or do not let its block end while the view is kept
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3314" for a longer explanation)

